# ----- MQTT ingest (agri-api-mqtt subscriber) ---------------------------------
# The subscriber consumes ChirpStack LoRaWAN uplinks straight off ChirpStack's
# own MQTT broker. Address that broker BY CONTAINER NAME — agri-api-mqtt joins
# ChirpStack's compose network (see docker-compose.yml `networks: chirpstack`),
# so the traffic never leaves docker.
#
# ⚠️ Do NOT use the docker bridge gateway IP (MQTT_HOST=172.18.0.1). That route
# exits the container to the HOST, so it is evaluated by the host INPUT chain:
# enabling UFW (policy DROP on INPUT) silently blackholed it — the socket sat in
# SYN_SENT, `mqtt.connected` was never logged, and LoRa ingest died while the
# container still claimed to be healthy. Container-to-container traffic bypasses
# INPUT entirely, so it cannot be firewalled off by mistake.
#
# ⚠️ Precedence: docker-compose.yml sets MQTT_HOST/MQTT_PORT on the service via
# `environment:` (${MQTT_HOST:-mosquitto}), which BEATS this env_file. So on a
# deployed host the effective value is the one in the COMPOSE-project .env next
# to docker-compose.yml (e.g. /root/agri-api/.env), not back/.env.
#
# Confirm the broker's real container name on the host with:
#   docker ps --format '{{.Names}}' | grep -i mosquitto
# Leave MQTT_HOST empty to disable the subscriber. Dev (this compose file) uses
# the bundled `mosquitto` service instead.
MQTT_HOST=chirpstack-mosquitto-1
MQTT_PORT=1883
MQTT_USERNAME=
MQTT_PASSWORD=
MQTT_TLS=false
# Liveness file the subscriber keeps fresh only WHILE connected; the container
# healthcheck stats it, so an unconnected subscriber reports unhealthy.
# MQTT_HEALTH_FILE=/tmp/mqtt-healthy
# MQTT_HEALTH_INTERVAL=15

